The battle system defines three core animation categories. See implementation details in:
- @sub-battle-animation-timing.mdc#Animation-Type-Timing (timing implementation)
- @sub-battle-visual-consistency.mdc#Animation-Categories (visual implementation)

1. Combat Animations
   - Must synchronize with battalion speed stats
   - Must maintain global state consistency

2. Movement Animations
   - Must maintain 60fps standard
   - Must follow network constraints

3. State Change Animations
   - Must provide clear visual feedback
   - Must handle error states appropriately

## Resource Management
// Core requirements defined in:
// - @battle-performance-standards.mdc#Animation-Performance#Animation-Optimization
// - @battle-state-persistence.mdc#Frontend-Performance

// Implementation details in:
// - @sub-battle-performance-boundaries.mdc#Resource-Management (optimization and monitoring)
// - @sub-battle-animation-timing.mdc#Technical-Implementation (timing)
// - @sub-battle-visual-consistency.mdc#Technical-Standards (visual)

The battle system requires strict resource management across three areas:

1. Optimization
   - Must use native drivers where possible
   - Must implement proper cleanup
   - Must batch similar operations
   See @sub-battle-performance-boundaries.mdc#Resource-Management#Optimization-Guidelines

2. Monitoring
   - Must track frame rates and performance
   - Must monitor resource usage
   - Must log errors and issues
   See @sub-battle-performance-boundaries.mdc#Resource-Management#Monitoring-Requirements

3. Resource Cleanup
   - Must handle unmount cleanup
   - Must verify resource release
   - Must maintain memory efficiency
   See @sub-battle-performance-boundaries.mdc#Resource-Management

Remember: All animations must maintain 60fps while respecting the global state management system. Proper cleanup and resource management are critical for sustained performance. 